@packages/cli import { SigilCli }
@integrations/skills/sigil/design-conversation.sigil import { SigilDesignConversation }
@integrations/skills/sigil/review-gate.sigil import { SigilReviewGate }

component SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  goal {
    Consume compiler-owned semantic-readiness and architecture evaluation as
    provisional evidence for exact written Sigil without transferring design
    decisions or implementation approval authority to the compiler.
  }

  interface {
DesignCompilationReview {
      evaluates exact written Sigil through ordinary SigilCli compilation focus
      design before user file review and any implementation ReviewGate

      consumes deterministic-foundation, semantic-readiness, and architecture-design
      results from the exact written scope

      permits written-file review when design compilation is green or when every
      yellow finding is explicitly reviewed and accepted as nonblocking by the human
      user
    }
  }
}

expand SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  state {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Design compilation is unavailable, incomplete, red, yellow, or green for one
      exact written scope and reviewed boundary.
    }
  }

  logic {
DesignCompilationReview {
      After design intent and applicable external guidance are sufficiently resolved,
      write the scoped Sigil change to the selected workspace and compile it with
      ordinary SigilCli focus design.

      Select the nearest importing module index whose retrieval closure covers all
      affected semantic units. When none exists, select the component whose closure
      covers the most changed units, matching expands, and direct relationships;
      break a tie by declaration proximity. Compile the workspace without a component
      when no component covers the boundary, and record every unit outside a partial
      target closure.

      Do not use a compiler session for normal authoring or review. A session remains
      available only for explicitly requested exceptional diagnostics.

      Design compilation may take substantial time. Wait for its terminal outcome:
      completed carries the authoritative version-2 CompilationReport, while failed
      and cancelled are terminal outcomes without a report.

      Before starting compilation, reserve and record a durable, task-scoped output
      path. Capture both compiler stdout and stderr there and preserve the command
      exit status. The capture must remain readable if the command tool returns
      before a spawned evaluator exits; it is the source of record, not merely a
      display copy of the live tool event.

      A stream consumer also waits for source end after the terminal event.
      Stage-started events, partial progress, silence while an evaluator runs, or a
      slow response do not establish red, yellow, or green evidence. Keep the agent
      session open while it runs. Do not cancel, abandon, or replace the same-scope
      compile because it is slow; a missing terminal is a host or transport problem.

      If the execution host interrupts the live stream, retrieve the durable capture
      and continue waiting from it. Poll its appended output and, when necessary, the
      evaluator process state until the capture contains a terminal event and its
      writer has closed. Preserve the final report or terminal diagnostics from that
      capture in review evidence. Do not replace the compile merely because the
      original live stream is unavailable. When the capture is inaccessible or cannot
      establish source end, report a host or transport failure and retry the same
      selected target only after restoring durable capture. Never classify partial
      output as a compiler result.

      Use red and yellow diagnostics to investigate evidence and return confirmed
      material problems to DesignConversation. Revise red candidates and yellow
      candidates with unresolved findings.

      After every semantic write, use the design report to choose one next action:
      correct a deterministic or coherence issue directly when established intent
      determines the correction, or enter DesignConversation when it exposes a
      material decision, conflicting intent, or future-facing pitfall.

      Write the scoped correction and recompile. Repeat until green or every yellow
      finding is explicitly reviewed and accepted as nonblocking. Do not continue to
      grouping, glossary extraction, or implementation from red, unresolved yellow,
      unavailable, or incomplete evidence.

      Present the exact green written scope, or a yellow scope whose every finding
      the human explicitly accepted as nonblocking, for user review in the file. Then
      continue to glossary extraction or implementation review.
    }
  }

  constraints {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Compiler green and reviewed yellow are provisional design evidence and do not
      approve glossary mutation or implementation.

      The skill does not duplicate semantic-readiness or architecture-design judgment
      already owned by the compiler focus closure.

      External-guidance applicability, design decisions, correction conversations,
      concept-grouping approval, glossary extraction, and ReviewGate remain owned by
      the host workflow.

      An unavailable, incomplete, or red required design compilation prevents
      implementation review. Yellow also prevents implementation readiness until the
      human explicitly reviews every finding and accepts it as nonblocking.

      A report that requires user judgment remains in DesignConversation; the host
      does not guess a correction merely to complete the compile-and-resolve loop.

      Written-file review evidence identifies the target workspace root, target
      paths, changed semantic units, target, profile, focus, and finding
      dispositions. It supports implementation review but never approves it.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilerDrivenDesignReview {
      Decision: Use SigilCompiler focus design as the semantic-readiness and
      modularity evaluator for exact written Sigil.

      Scope: Governs SigilSkill design review after scoped Sigil is written; excludes
      human design decisions, external-guidance research, glossary extraction, and
      implementation approval.

      Trade-offs: Semantic work is immediately visible in the target file, so users
      review the actual change rather than a chat proposal, but compilation may take
      substantial time before it yields terminal evidence.

      Design issues addressed: Temporary proposal sessions consume context and make
      small-model review harder to follow.

      Discarded alternatives: Retaining duplicate host semantic and modularity review
      was rejected because the compiler design closure already owns those
      evaluations.

      Consequences: One ordinary compiler report evaluates deterministic validity,
      semantic readiness, and architecture on the file the user reviews.
    }

    ReviewedYellowDesignEvidence {
      Decision: Permit a complete yellow design compilation to proceed only after the
      human explicitly reviews and accepts every finding as nonblocking.

      Scope: Governs provisional design evidence for Sigil proposal, glossary, and
      implementation workflows; excludes unavailable, incomplete, and red reports.

      Trade-offs: Noncritical warnings may remain intentionally deferred, and their
      dispositions must travel with the exact report evidence.

      Design issues addressed: Treating every warning as blocking prevents informed
      human judgment, while treating yellow as automatic readiness hides unresolved
      findings.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic rejection and automatic acceptance of every
      yellow report were rejected.

      Consequences: Reviewed warnings remain visible and scoped to one report; new or
      changed findings require another disposition.
    }
  }

  cases {
DesignCompilationReview {
      A red written Sigil with contradictory state ownership returns to
      DesignConversation before concept grouping or glossary extraction.

      A yellow written scope with one unresolved warning remains blocked. When a
      human explicitly accepts every finding as nonblocking, its report and
      dispositions may proceed as provisional evidence.

      An ungrouped green written scope proceeds to concept grouping, then recompiles
      before glossary extraction.

      An unavailable compilation reports the unavailable compiler evidence and may
      retry only after capability is restored.

      An incomplete compilation reports completed and missing stages plus available
      budget or evidence limitations, blocks implementation review, and permits a
      retry after the limitation is resolved.
    }
  }
}
